      "content": "The High Court in Vaughan v. Maritime Logistics Ltd [2022] held that the defendant's failure to maintain traction sensors on cargo vessels constituted a breach of the duty of care under the Merchant Shipping Act 1995. This decision built upon established principles of maritime negligence, which have evolved from the seminal case of Donoghue v Stevenson [1932], where the House of Lords established the general duty of care owed by a merchant to those who may be foreseeably harmed by their actions. The court's application of the three-stage test from Caparo Industries plc v Dickman [1990] - namely, that the defendant must have knowledge of the risk, take steps to mitigate that risk, and fail to do so - was crucial in determining the defendant's breach of duty. Furthermore, the court's consideration of the application of existing precedents in Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police [2018] reinforced the importance of adhering to established negligence duties in maritime contexts, and was consistent with the principles outlined in the Supreme Court's decision in Walford v British Railways Board [1988], which emphasized the need for a defendant to take reasonable care to avoid acts or omissions that may foreseeably cause harm to others. Ultimately",
